// Acquisition strictly increases, so circular wait is impossible (D-056, 0.5.6).
//
// WHY THIS ANALYSIS EXISTS. `--verify-concurrency` was documented as verifying
// "data race AND deadlock freedom", and deadlock freedom had no mechanism
// anywhere. A flag promising a safety property nothing delivers is worse than
// promising nothing, because it invites reliance — and the person relying on it is
// deciding whether a robot near a child can wedge.
//
// THE ROOT CAUSE WAS TYPE ERASURE. The prototype's `func:lock = int32(int64:handle)`
// says nothing about WHICH lock is taken, so no analysis could order acquisitions
// and no reviewer could either. The fix is the one taken for `printf` and `ioctl`:
// put the information in the type, and `acquires N` is what carries it into the
// signature.
//
// WHAT MARKS AN ACQUISITION IS A CONTRACT, NOT A NAME. These fixtures declare
// their own levelled functions, because the concurrency stdlib does not exist yet
// — which is the right order: the analysis is what tells us the shape is
// checkable. Recognising an acquisition by its SPELLING is what the prototype did
// for deallocators and what D-119 refused.

mod:lock_levels;
error:E9;

struct:Lease = { int32:v; };

func:take_1 = Lease() acquires 1i32 { pass Lease{ v: 1i32 }; };
func:take_2 = Lease() acquires 2i32 { pass Lease{ v: 2i32 }; };
func:take_3 = Lease() acquires 3i32 { pass Lease{ v: 3i32 }; };
func:take_9 = Lease() acquires 9i32 { pass Lease{ v: 9i32 }; };

// --- acquiring downward -----------------------------------------------------------

// expect-error: NITPICK-LOCK-001
func:downward = NIL() never fails {
    Lease:a = take_2() ?! E9;
    Lease:b = take_1() ?! E9;
    discard(a.v);
    discard(b.v);
    pass NIL;
};

// STRICTLY increasing, so the same level twice is refused too. Two threads taking
// the same lock in the other order is the classic cycle, and "greater than or
// equal" would permit exactly that.
// expect-error: NITPICK-LOCK-001
func:same_level_twice = NIL() never fails {
    Lease:a = take_2() ?! E9;
    Lease:b = take_2() ?! E9;
    discard(a.v);
    discard(b.v);
    pass NIL;
};

// --- through the call graph ----------------------------------------------------------
//
// The violation is not visible in either function on its own, which is why the
// analysis is whole-program. `reaches_1` looks fine; holding 3 and calling it is
// not.

func:reaches_1 = NIL() never fails {
    Lease:g = take_1() ?! E9;
    discard(g.v);
    pass NIL;
};

// expect-error: NITPICK-LOCK-001
func:transitively_downward = NIL() never fails {
    Lease:a = take_3() ?! E9;
    discard(a.v);
    drop reaches_1();
    pass NIL;
};

// A FUNCTION'S OWN DECLARED LEVEL IS HELD INSIDE ITS BODY. Saying `acquires 2i32`
// means everything below happens with 2 held, so a helper reaching 1 is downward.
// expect-error: NITPICK-LOCK-001
func:declares_two_calls_one = NIL() acquires 2i32 {
    drop reaches_1();
    pass NIL;
};

// --- a trait method's bound, and the implementation that breaks it ----------------------
//
// A call through a trait object can reach anything, so its acquisition set would be
// unbounded. D-056's answer is that the method DECLARES a maximum and
// implementations are checked against it — which is what makes the whole-program
// walk terminate at a trait object instead of giving up or under-approximating.

trait:Storage = {
    func:commit = NIL(Self:self) acquires <= 3i32;
};

struct:Loose = { int32:v; };

// THE IMPLEMENTATION DOES NOT REPEAT THE CLAUSE, and is bound by it anyway.
// Requiring the repetition would make the promise opt-in at the one place it has to
// be kept.
// expect-error: NITPICK-LOCK-002
impl:Loose:Storage = {
    pub func:commit = NIL(Self:self) never fails {
        Lease:g = take_9() ?! E9;
        discard(g.v);
        pass NIL;
    };
};

// --- a level that is not a constant ------------------------------------------------------

// `acquires (1i32 + 1i32)` FOLDS since 0.6.4, and so does `acquires LEVEL` for a
// `const` global — the same evaluator an array size uses.
//
// WHAT IS STILL REFUSED IS THE RULE RATHER THAN THE RUNG: a lock level has to be
// a value the compiler knows, because the ordering is decided while compiling or
// it is not decided at all.
//
// A PARAMETER is the shape that says so now. This case named a module binding
// until 1.4.2c, when `const` retired: every module binding is a compile-time
// constant by construction (D-165 requires the initialiser to be one, D-211
// requires the qualifier), so one standing in a lock level folds and is legal.
// A parameter's value arrives at run time and cannot.

// expect-error: NITPICK-LOCK-003
func:level_is_not_constant = NIL(int32:runtime_level) acquires runtime_level {
    pass NIL;
};

// --- the TYPED acquisition (D-056, 1.1.11) -----------------------------------------
//
// `m.acquire(…)` carries its level in the RECEIVER'S TYPE — the 0.5.6
// doctrine's own fix arriving: no clause is needed at the site, and the
// ordering rule reads the level off `Mutex<T, LEVEL>` directly. Holding 5
// and acquiring 3 is the downward acquisition that makes circular wait
// possible, wherever the level came from.
// expect-error: NITPICK-LOCK-001
async func:typed_downward = NIL(Mutex<int32, 3i32>->:low) acquires 5i32 {
    Guard<int32>:g = relay await low.acquire(raw duration_ms(5i64));
    discard(g.value);
    pass NIL;
};
